02:04:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you feel that way. When a law is literally named the "Freedom of Information Act," it sets a very clear expectation: information should be free and accessible. It is incredibly frustrating to invoke a law with that name only to receive a document covered in black ink. However, there is often a big difference between the title of a law and the actual legal text inside it. The title of the law represents its *default baseline*: that government records belong to the public. But the actual text of the law, which Congress wrote and passed in 1966, has *always* included exemptions. It was never written with the intention that the government must hand over absolutely everything requested. While it might seem like common sense that a transparency law should have no exceptions, absolute transparency quickly collides with other "common sense" realities. The federal government holds a massive amount of data. If FOIA had zero exceptions and mandated that agencies hand over *whatever* anyone asked for: * **Your privacy would be gone:** Anyone could use FOIA to request your personal tax returns from the IRS, your medical records from the VA or Medicare, or your Social Security number. * **Security would be compromised:** A foreign adversary could legally request the blueprints to the Pentagon, the passwords to federal cybersecurity systems, or the names and locations of undercover agents. * **Safety would be at risk:** Anyone could request the home addresses of federal judges, prosecutors, or FBI agents. Because a "no exceptions" rule would lead to those kinds of disasters, lawmakers had to build boundaries into the law from day one. It is entirely fair to criticize how the government *applies* the law. Transparency advocates and journalists argue every single day that federal agencies abuse these exemptions, using them as a shield to hide embarrassing mistakes, cover up corruption, or just avoid the hard work of finding the